gaao(){return"What science says"},
gaak(){return"Burning tobacco exposes people to carbon monoxide, particles, and many toxic substances. Nicotine mainly sustains dependence and is not risk-free."},
gaal(){return"Nicotine replacement therapy is among the recommended aids for stopping or reducing tobacco. Choose the product, dose, and duration with a doctor or pharmacist."},
gaam(){return"Health benefits appear at different rates after stopping tobacco. Health milestone timings are population averages from institutional sources, not guarantees."},
gaan(){return"Even a brief return to smoking can reactivate learned routines. Planning for triggers and seeking support are more useful than relying on willpower alone."},
ga9W(){return"Safety first"},
ga9V(){return"If you drink every day or in large amounts, or have previously had withdrawal symptoms, do not change your intake on your own: contact a doctor or addiction service promptly. Confusion, hallucinations, seizures, severe illness, or severe agitation are emergencies: call 15 or 112 in France, or your local emergency number. Respire does not determine a withdrawal plan."},
ga9X(){return"What science says"},
ga9R(){return"Any alcohol use carries risk. French lower-risk guidance is no more than 10 standard drinks per week, no more than 2 per day, with alcohol-free days."},
ga9S(){return"Alcohol may make it easier to fall asleep while reducing sleep quality and increasing awakenings. Changes after reducing or stopping vary; persistent symptoms should be discussed with a professional."},
ga9T(){return"In France, one standard drink contains about 10 g of pure alcohol: 250 ml of 5% beer, 100 ml of 12% wine, or 30 ml of 40% spirits. A generous serving may count as several standard drinks."},
ga9U(){return"Reducing alcohol lowers health risks. The changes you notice and their timing depend on previous intake and health status."},
ga9o(){return"The loop"},
ga9n(){return"A joint mixed with tobacco combines nicotine, THC, and combustion products. Each can affect dependence, alertness, sleep, and airways differently; the journal can reveal associations but cannot establish a medical cause."},
ga9q(){return"The sleep paradox"},
ga9p(){return"The effects of cannabis and THC on sleep depend on dose, frequency, product, and the individual. Frequent use may lead to tolerance; reducing or stopping may temporarily disturb sleep. Cannabis is not a treatment for insomnia to use without medical advice."},
ga9x(){return"Medical use: keep a clinical framework"},
ga9r(){return"Some cannabinoid medicines may be considered for specific indications after medical assessment. Self-medication is not equivalent to prescribed treatment."},
ga9s(){return"Sleep evidence is limited and mainly concerns selected medical conditions and short follow-up periods."},
ga9t(){return"For some forms of spasticity, cannabinoid medicines may provide modest benefit for some patients; response and adverse effects vary."},
ga9u(){return"THC may feel relaxing to some people but can also cause anxiety, panic, or psychotic symptoms, especially with high-THC products."},
ga9v(){return"Smoking cannabis exposes the lungs to harmful substances. Vaping is not risk-free: aerosols may contain irritants or metals."},
ga9w(){return"Broad comparisons between cannabis and alcohol hide different risks. Route, frequency, age, medical history, and combined substance use all matter."},
ga9F(){return"Risks to know"},
ga9y(){return"Frequent use may be associated with sleep problems. Insomnia and vivid dreams can occur temporarily after reducing or stopping."},
ga9z(){return"If pain appears or worsens during a reduction, do not automatically attribute it to withdrawal: seek medical advice, especially if it is severe or persistent."},
ga9A(){return"Cannabis can acutely impair attention, memory, coordination, and reaction time. Long-term risks increase with early onset and frequent use."},
ga9B(){return"Cannabis can increase heart rate and poses particular concerns for people with cardiovascular disease. Ask a professional if this applies to you."},
ga9C(){return"Smoking cannabis is associated with cough, phlegm, and bronchitis; combining it with tobacco adds the risks of both types of smoke."},
ga9D(){return"Cannabis can lead to dependence, especially with frequent use or use beginning at a young age. An addiction professional can offer tailored support."},
ga9E(){return"Repeated nausea or vomiting in a regular cannabis user may suggest cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome and warrants medical assessment."},
ga9H(){return u.qh},
ga9G(){return"Tolerance can develop with repeated use: less effect from the same amount or a perceived need to increase it. Relief between uses may also partly reflect withdrawal symptoms easing."},
ga9J(){return"The nuance that works in your favor"},
ga9I(){return"Chronic pain or a sleep disorder deserves its own assessment, independently of substance use. Treating it with a professional may make reduction more comfortable and avoid masking another cause."},
ga9L(){return"Sleep"},
ga9K(){return"Regular schedules, morning light, a quiet and cool bedroom, and fewer screens near bedtime may support sleep. Persistent insomnia, breathing pauses, or marked daytime sleepiness warrant medical advice; CBT-I is a first-line treatment for chronic insomnia."},
ga9Q(){return"Physical recovery"},
ga9M(){return"Choose regular, varied meals. Specific protein needs should be assessed according to age, activity, and health status."},
ga9N(){return"Do not start creatine or another supplement for withdrawal without checking its value, contraindications, and interactions with a professional."},
ga9O(){return"Vitamin D testing or supplementation should not be automatic. Excess supplementation can be toxic; follow professional advice."},
ga9P(){return"Drink fluids regularly. Seek prompt medical advice for heavy sweating, vomiting, confusion, marked weakness, or inability to drink."},
ga9m(){return"Signs worth discussing with a doctor"},
ga9i(){return u.gC},
ga9j(){return u.w1},
ga9k(){return"Tremors, sweating, nausea, agitation, or marked anxiety after reducing alcohol may indicate withdrawal: contact a doctor promptly. Confusion, hallucinations, or seizures require emergency help."},
